          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "If someone has COPD and an exacerbation, is that one code or two?",
          "answer": "When someone has COPD with an exacerbation, it typically involves more than one ICD-10 code if both an infection and an exacerbation are documented. The primary code for COPD is J44.\n\nAccording to the document \"Coding chronic obstructive pulmonary disease: J44 and its two complication codes,\" an infection and an exacerbation are considered different events, and if both are present, both are reported. This differs from how asthma exacerbations are coded, where exacerbation and status asthmaticus are mutually exclusive.\n\nFurthermore, the infection code for COPD with an exacerbation indicates the presence of an infection but does not specify the type. A second code is needed to identify the specific organism or infection, which would come from the relevant ICD-10 category.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
